//
// SMYSL and logic programming in Bund, together: why the village well at
// Ashby ran dry. A SMYSL library is formed from a text, from SMYSL's own
// syntax and from Bund MAPs; it is checked, researched, measured and
// queried; then it becomes a knowledge base, rules reason over it, and
// smysl.prove writes each proof back into it as units a later query, rule
// or proof can use. Run it from a directory with no `ashby` in it:
//
//   bund2 demo.bund
//
// It needs a bund2 built with `--features smysl`. It writes its library to
// `ashby` beside where it runs; a second run would find the first's units
// and proofs there and print otherwise, so the Makefile and the test both
// start from none. It prints no clock and no path. `expected.txt` is what
// it prints, and `expected.stderr.txt` the three warnings raised along
// the way (sections 4 and 6).
//

//
// 1. A library, and a text in it. `smysl.create` makes the library and
//    answers its handle. `smysl.text.add` reads a Markdown text through
//    SMYSL's `md/1` reader under an alias, and answers its manifest id,
//    its parts, and whether anything was lost or withheld. Adding the same
//    text again answers the same manifest. `smysl.texts` lists each alias.
//
"== 1. a library, and a text in it" println
:lib "ashby" smysl.create var
:survey_text "# Ashby water survey

The village well at Ashby ran dry in August. The water table under the
green fell by two metres over the summer.

## The brewery

The new brewery on the Ashby road draws from a borehole forty metres deep,
and began pumping in June.

## The weather

Rainfall from May to August was half the ten-year mean.
" var
:survey :lib var?
  dict :reader "md/1" set :alias "survey" set :licence "CC0-1.0" set :lang "en" set
       :title "Ashby water survey" set :text :survey_text var? set
  smysl.text.add var
"parts read from the text:  " print :survey var? :parts get len println drop
"lossy, redacted:           " print :survey var? :lossy get print ", " print :survey var? :redacted get println
"aliases in the library:    " print :lib var? smysl.texts :survey get len println drop
"added again, same manifest: " print
:lib var?
  dict :reader "md/1" set :alias "survey" set :licence "CC0-1.0" set :lang "en" set
       :title "Ashby water survey" set :text :survey_text var? set
  smysl.text.add :mid get :survey var? :mid get == println
"" println

//
// 2. Forming from SMYSL's syntax. `smysl.parse` reads a document into the
//    MAPs Bund2 uses for units, relations and labels, with the document's
//    labels (`o/dry`) resolved; `smysl.append` writes the whole LIST under
//    one acquisition of the library's locks, and answers each record's id.
//    `x.text/entity` is SMYSL's entity type: section 6's dossier is built
//    around one, through the units that mention it.
//
"== 2. forming from SMYSL's syntax" println
:doc '@x.text/entity e/ashby { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:survey" } }
~ Ashby

@question q/why { status: speculative }
~ Why did the Ashby well run dry?

@observation o/dry { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "Parish log, 28 August" } }
~ The village well at Ashby ran dry in August.

@observation o/table { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "Ashby water survey" } }
~ The water table under the green fell two metres over the summer.

@observation o/borehole { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "Brewery licence" } }
~ The brewery borehole draws from forty metres down and began pumping in June.

@observation o/rain { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "County rain gauge" } }
~ Rainfall from May to August was half the ten-year mean.

@claim c/drought { status: inferred, grounds: [o/rain, o/table] }
~ The summer drought lowered the water table.

@claim c/brewery { status: inferred, grounds: [o/borehole, o/table] }
~ The brewery borehole drew the water table down.

@claim c/weather { status: speculative }
~ The weather alone dried the well.

@rel c/drought --answers--> q/why
@rel c/brewery --answers--> q/why
@rel c/drought --causes--> o/dry
@rel c/brewery --causes--> o/dry
@rel c/brewery --rebuts--> c/weather
@rel c/drought --elaborates--> c/weather
@rel q/why --x.text/mentions--> e/ashby
@rel o/dry --x.text/mentions--> e/ashby
@rel o/table --x.text/mentions--> e/ashby
@rel c/drought --x.text/mentions--> e/ashby
@rel c/brewery --x.text/mentions--> e/ashby
@rel c/weather --x.text/mentions--> e/ashby
' var
:records :doc var? smysl.parse var
"records parsed:            " print :records var? len println drop
"ids appended:              " print :lib var? :records var? smysl.append len println drop
"" println

//
// 3. Forming from MAPs. The library's own facts (section 8) give each
//    label's uid, gathered here into a MAP. A unit MAP may name its
//    grounds by label; `smysl.label` binds one; `smysl.rel` writes an
//    edge. `smysl.append` takes any LIST of MAPs, built here with `map`.
//
"== 3. forming from MAPs, naming units by label" println
:labels dict var
:lib var? "from library" smysl.kb [ label L U ] kb.query
  { :b swap var :labels :labels var? :b var? :L get :b var? :U get set var } loop
"labels the library binds:  " print :labels var? len println drop

:refill :lib var?
  dict :schema "observation" set
       :gist   "The brewery stopped pumping on 1 September, and the well refilled within ten days." set
       :status "cited" set
       :source dict :kind "doc" set :reference "Parish log, 12 September" set set
  smysl.unit var
:lib var? "o/refill" :refill var? smysl.label
:pumping :lib var?
  dict :schema "claim" set
       :gist   "Pumping at the brewery, more than the drought, emptied the well." set
       :status "inferred" set
       :grounds [ "o/borehole" "o/refill" "o/table" ] set
  smysl.unit var
:lib var? "c/pumping" :pumping var? smysl.label
:lib var? dict :kind "rebuts" set :from :pumping var? set :to :labels var? "c/weather" get set smysl.rel drop
:lib var? dict :kind "answers" set :from :pumping var? set :to :labels var? "q/why" get set smysl.rel drop

"two more questions, one LIST of MAPs:" println
:asked :lib var?
  [ "Will the well fail again next summer?" "Should the brewery licence cap its pumping?" ]
  { :g swap var dict :schema "question" set :gist :g var? set :status "speculative" set } map
  smysl.append var
:asked var? { "  " print :lib var? swap smysl.get :gist get println } loop
"" println

//
// 4. Checking, and the library as text. `smysl.check` runs SMYSL's checks
//    and answers each diagnostic as a MAP: none here. The `x.text` edges
//    section 2 wrote would draw `SMY-W013` from an undeclared schema, but a
//    batch that writes an `x.text` record carries SMYSL's declaration of
//    it, as SMYSL's own text ingest does. `smysl.write` gives the library
//    back as SMYSL's syntax, the declaration with it; parsing it again
//    warns that the declaration and the text's manifest, records with no
//    MAP form, are skipped.
//
"== 4. checking, and the library as text" println
"diagnostics from SMYSL's checks: " print :lib var? smysl.check len println drop
:lib var? smysl.check { "  " print dup :code get print " " print :message get println } loop
:written :lib var? smysl.write var
"lines of SMYSL's syntax:   " print :written var? string.tokenize.lines len println drop
"records it parses back to: " print :written var? smysl.parse len println drop
"" println

//
// 5. Researching. `smysl.trace` follows a unit's lineage: `rests-on` its
//    premises, `dependents` what rests on it, to any depth with NODATA.
//    `smysl.salience` ranks the units; `smysl.pack` chooses what fits a
//    token budget.
//
"== 5. researching" println
"what the brewery claim rests on:" println
:lib var? :labels var? "c/brewery" get "rests-on" ∅ smysl.trace
  { "  " print dup :depth get print " " print :uid get :lib var? swap smysl.get :gist get println } loop
"what depends on the falling water table:" println
:lib var? :labels var? "o/table" get "dependents" ∅ smysl.trace
  { "  " print dup :depth get print " " print :uid get :lib var? swap smysl.get :gist get println } loop
"the three most salient units:" println
:lib var? dict smysl.salience 3 swap head
  { "  " print :uid get :lib var? swap smysl.get :gist get println } loop
"what fits in 60 tokens:" println
:lib var? dict :budget 60 set smysl.pack
  { "  " print :lib var? swap smysl.get :gist get println } loop
"" println

//
// 6. A dossier, and measures. `smysl.dossier` asks SMYSL to gather what
//    the library holds about a question's entity into named slots.
//    `smysl.measure` runs SMYSL's measures over a scope: T1 needs more text
//    than one survey, so it answers why it did not run, and SMYSL's reason
//    is also a warning; A4 measures the spread of the claims' statuses.
//
"== 6. a dossier, and measures" println
:dossier :lib var? "Why did the Ashby well run dry?" smysl.dossier var
"the dossier's subject:     " print :dossier var? :subject get println
:dossier var? :slots get
  { "  " print dup :name get print ":" println
    :units get { "    " print :lib var? swap smysl.get :gist get println } loop } loop
:lib var? [ "T1" ] "from texts" smysl.measure
  { "  " print dup :id get print " " print dup :ok get print ": " print :value get println } loop
:lib var? [ "A4" ] "from library
where kind = claim" smysl.measure
  { "  " print dup :id get print " " print dup :ok get print ": " print :value get println } loop
"" println

//
// 7. Querying. `smysl.query` runs one SMYSL query and answers a MAP whose
//    `kind` says its shape: a count, groups, a ranking. The `smysl`
//    conditional runs a lambda once for each unit a query names, with the
//    unit's MAP on top.
//
"== 7. querying" println
"units:                     " print :lib var? "from library
count" smysl.query :count get println
"by kind:" println
:lib var? "from library
group by kind" smysl.query :groups get
  { "  " print dup :key get print " " print :units get len println drop } loop
'ranked for "brewery":' println
:lib var? 'from library
find "brewery"' smysl.query dup :kind get "  answered as " print println
  :hits get { "  " print :uid get :lib var? swap smysl.get :gist get println } loop
"the claims, one at a time:" println
smysl
  :handle :lib var? set
  :query  "from library
where kind = claim" set
  :lambda { "  " print dup :status get print " " print :gist get println } set
!
"" println

//
// 8. The library as a knowledge base. `smysl.kb` turns the units a query
//    names into facts for the `kb.` words: `unit`, `gist`, `grounds`,
//    `deps`, `label`, `rel`, `effective` and `rank`. A LIST of goals asks
//    them together. `rank` facts are integers, so CLP(FD) constrains them.
//    The `query` conditional runs a lambda per answer, here over a KB of
//    the observations alone.
//
"== 8. the library as a knowledge base" println
:facts :lib var? "from library" smysl.kb var
"facts the library gives:   " print :facts var? kb.clauses len println drop
"the claims, with their status, asked of the facts:" println
:facts var? [ [ unit U claim S ] [ gist U G ] ] kb.query
  { "  " print dup :S get print " " print :G get println } loop
"does anything rebut a claim: " print :facts var? [ rel rebuts X Y ] kb.holds println
"statuses ranked at least derived, by CLP(FD):" println
:facts var? [ [ rank S R ] [ #>= R 3 ] ] kb.query { "  " print dup :S get print " " print :R get println } loop
"the observations alone, through the query conditional:" println
query
  :kb     :lib var? "from library
where kind = observation" smysl.kb set
  :goal   [ gist U G ] set
  :lambda { "  " print :G get println } set
!
"" println

//
// 9. Rules over the library. Rules merge with the facts as with any KB:
//    - `rests_on` is left recursive, so it is tabled;
//    - `is` brings a Bund value into a goal, and `kb.word` makes a
//      predicate a word, NODATA (`∅`) standing for what it finds;
//    - `test` keeps an answer when a Bund lambda says so, and `is` binds
//      what a lambda answers;
//    - `in` ranges over a LIST a lambda answers: here `smysl.trace`, so a
//      rule reads the library live while it is being solved;
//    - `kb.saturate` derives every fact the rules allow.
//
"== 9. rules over the library" println
:rules [
  [ [ rests_on U G ] ← [ grounds U G ] ]
  [ [ rests_on U G ] ← [ rests_on U M ] [ grounds M G ] ]
  [ [ explains C Q ] ← [ rel answers C Q ] ]
  [ [ about_water U ] ← [ gist U G ] [ test { "*water*" G string.wildcard } ] ]
  [ [ words U N ] ← [ gist U G ] [ is N { G string.tokenize len swap drop } ] ]
  [ [ upstream U X ] ← [ in X { :lib var? U "grounds" ∅ smysl.trace { :uid get } map } ] ]
] kb! :rests_on 2 kb.table var
:kb :facts var? :rules var? kb.merge var
:why :labels var? "q/why" get var
:pumped :labels var? "c/brewery" get var

"what the brewery claim rests on, tabled:" println
:kb var? [ [ is P { :pumped var? } ] [ rests_on P G ] [ gist G T ] ] kb.query
  { "  " print :T get println } loop
"what answers why the well ran dry, through a word made of a predicate:" println
:kb :explains 2 kb.word
∅ :why var? explains { "  " print 1 swap at :lib var? swap smysl.get :gist get println } loop
"units about water, by a Bund test inside a rule:" println
:kb var? [ [ about_water U ] [ gist U G ] ] kb.query { "  " print :G get println } loop
"each claim's length in words, a value a lambda answers:" println
:kb var? [ [ unit U claim S ] [ words U N ] [ gist U G ] ] kb.query
  { "  " print dup :N get print " " print :G get println } loop
"upstream of the pumping claim, generated by smysl.trace inside a rule:" println
:kb var? [ [ is P { :pumping var? } ] [ upstream P X ] [ gist X G ] ] kb.query
  { "  " print :G get println } loop
:closure :facts var? [
  [ [ rests_on U G ] ← [ grounds U G ] ]
  [ [ rests_on U G ] ← [ rests_on U M ] [ grounds M G ] ]
] kb! kb.merge var
"rests_on facts saturating derives: " print
:closure var? kb.clauses len swap drop :closure var? kb.saturate kb.clauses len swap drop - println
"" println

//
// 10. Asking what the library does not hold. `kb.askable` sends a goal no
//     fact proves to an `ask` lambda, with replies remembered in `known`;
//     both are seeded here, so nothing is read from the terminal.
//     `smysl.prove` has no `ask` slot, so a proof that needs a reply is
//     refused, and nothing is written.
//
"== 10. asking what the library does not hold" println
:advice :facts var? [
  [ [ act C ] ← [ rel answers C Q ] [ rel rebuts C W ] [ dry_summer ] ]
] kb! kb.merge :dry_summer 0 kb.askable var
:replies [ [ [ dry_summer ] true ] ] var
query
  :kb     :advice var? set
  :goal   [ act C ] set
  :known  :replies set
  :ask    { "{0}? (y/n) " format input "y" == } set
  :lambda { "  act on: " print :C get :lib var? swap smysl.get :gist get println } set
!
?try
  :try    { :lib var? :advice var? [ act C ] dict smysl.prove println } set
  :except { "smysl.prove has no one to ask: " print :context get println } set
!
"" println

//
// 11. Proofs written into the library. `smysl.prove` proves a goal against
//     a KB and writes each proof back: a fact the library bears out stands
//     for its units; a rule's conclusion becomes a claim grounded on what
//     its premises became; the root is a `finding`. A conclusion is never
//     stronger than its weakest ground, so a culprit resting on a
//     speculative claim is speculative. A fact only the program holds,
//     `licence_cap`, is written as a claim at the status `opts.facts`
//     names, here `cited`, with the source `opts.source` gives.
//
"== 11. proofs written into the library" println
:theory [
  [ [ explains C Q ] ← [ rel answers C Q ] ]
  [ [ culprit C ] ← [ explains C Q ] [ rel rebuts C W ] ]
  [ [ grounded C ] ← [ unit C claim inferred ] [ once [ grounds C G ] [ unit G observation cited ] ] ]
  [ [ open Q ] ← [ unit Q question S ] [ not [ rel answers C Q ] ] ]
  [ [ needs_cap C ] ← [ culprit C ] [ licence_cap L ] ]
  [ licence_cap 2000 ]
] kb! var
:show {
  dup :schema get print " " print dup :status get print ": " print dup :gist get println
} register
:prove {
  :goal swap var :opts swap var
  :lib var? :lib var? "from library" smysl.kb :theory var? kb.merge :goal var? :opts var? smysl.prove
  { :lib var? swap smysl.get "  " print show
    :grounds get { :lib var? swap smysl.get "    on " print show drop } loop } loop
} register
"culprits, each a finding at the status its grounds allow:" println
dict [ culprit C ] prove
"claims grounded on a cited observation:" println
dict [ grounded C ] prove
"questions nothing answers, proved by negation:" println
dict [ open Q ] prove
"a program fact authored as cited, with its source:" println
dict :facts "cited" set :source dict :kind "doc" set :reference "Brewery licence, clause 4" set set
  [ needs_cap C ] prove
"findings in the library now: " print :lib var? "from library
where kind = finding
count" smysl.query :count get println
"" println

//
// 12. Reasoning over the proofs. The findings are units like any other:
//     the next `smysl.kb` gives facts about them, a rule can rest on them,
//     and the proof it writes is grounded on them.
//
"== 12. reasoning over the proofs" println
:review [
  [ [ settled Q ] ← [ rel answers C Q ] [ unit F finding S ] [ grounds F C ] [ gist F G ]
                    [ test { "*culprit*" G string.wildcard } ] ]
] kb! var
"a question settled by a finding the last section wrote:" println
:lib var? :lib var? "from library" smysl.kb :review var? kb.merge [ settled Q ] dict smysl.prove
  { :lib var? swap smysl.get "  " print show
    :grounds get { :lib var? swap smysl.get "    on " print show drop } loop } loop
"" println

//
// 13. A retraction, and what the facts then say. `smysl.retract` retracts
//     a unit as SMYSL's own `retract` does. Under SMYSL's strict policy
//     only that unit's effective status changes, and `smysl.kb` gives it
//     as an `effective` fact. A rule that reads it finds the claims the
//     retraction shakes, and the proof is written. Until a retraction
//     exists there is no `effective` fact, so the rules declare it.
//
"== 13. a retraction, and what the facts then say" println
:shaken [
  [ [ shaken C ] ← [ grounds C G ] [ effective G unfounded ] [ unit C claim S ] ]
  [ [ effective U S ] ← [ fail ] ]
] kb! var
"before: is any claim shaken: " print
:lib var? "from library" smysl.kb :shaken var? kb.merge [ shaken C ] kb.holds println
"the licence shows a twenty-metre borehole; its record is retracted" println
:lib var? :labels var? "o/borehole" get smysl.retract drop
"units smysl.status says retraction changed: " print :lib var? smysl.status len println drop
"the same, as effective facts:" println
:lib var? "from library" smysl.kb [ [ effective U S ] [ gist U G ] ] kb.query
  { "  " print dup :S get print " " print :G get println } loop
"after: the claims it shakes, proved and written:" println
:lib var? :lib var? "from library" smysl.kb :shaken var? kb.merge [ shaken C ] dict smysl.prove
  { :lib var? swap smysl.get "  " print show drop } loop
"" println

//
// 14. What the words refuse, each caught by `?try`: a label already bound
//     to another unit (SM08), a `retracts` edge written by hand (SM03),
//     an unknown trace kind (SM05), and a MAP that is not a handle (SM00).
//
"== 14. what the words refuse" println
?try
  :try    { :lib var? "o/dry" :refill var? smysl.label } set
  :except { "a label bound elsewhere:   " print :context get println } set
!
?try
  :try    { :lib var? dict :kind "retracts" set :from :refill var? set :to :refill var? set smysl.rel } set
  :except { "a retracts edge by hand:   " print :context get println } set
!
?try
  :try    { :lib var? :refill var? "sideways" ∅ smysl.trace } set
  :except { "an unknown trace kind:     " print :context get println } set
!
?try
  :try    { dict :root "ashby" set :refill var? smysl.get } set
  :except { "a MAP that is no handle:   " print :context get println } set
!
"" println
"units in the library at the end: " print :lib var? "from library
count" smysl.query :count get println
